; langswitch.eval - a console language switch mid-game leaves the game where it was.
;
; Run:  Dungeon.exe -headless -eval <path to this file>
; Exit: 0 if every line ran, nothing was refused, and it ended playing.
;
; WHY IT EXISTS. On 2026-09-24 a scripted UI audit switched language with `lang`
; while the map editor was open, and a few commands later every in-game command
; answered "only works in-game". The switch was blamed. It was innocent: the
; audit ran in a FRESH world, where Start New Game puts the party three squares
; from crypt1's skeleton, and the world keeps simulating while the console is
; open - the idle party was beaten down and the wipe sent the game to the title.
; The wipe was silent in dungeon.log (its message reached only the HUD), so the
; log read as the game losing its state on its own. Two things came out of it:
; the wipe now logs "back to the title: the party fell", and the in-game guards
; REFUSE rather than print - which is what gives this script teeth, since a
; refused line fails the verdict.
;
; What it pins down: the language rebuild (GameUI::RebuildForLanguage, deferred
; a frame by Game::m_pendingLanguage) touches no state. The `state` lines are
; the record; the guarded commands after each switch are the check.
;
; NOTE `lang` persists to settings.ini (it is the Settings dropdown's path), so
; this ends on `lang en`. A run that dies part way leaves the language switched.

logecho on
reset
state

; The shape of the original: the editor up when the switch lands.
editor
validate
state

lang de
state
validate
state
validate

; A second switch, and one with the overlay back in player mode.
editor off
lang es
state
validate

lang en
state
validate
state
